[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is the fourth principle of the SOLID acronym. It states that no client should be forced to depend on methods it does not use. In object-oriented design, ISP focuses on splitting large, monolithic interfaces into smaller, more specific ones so that clients only need to know about the methods that are relevant to them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem with Fat Interfaces"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A "fat" interface is an interface that contains too many methods, often mixing unrelated responsibilities. When classes implement a fat interface, they are forced to define behaviors they do not actually need. This leads to "NotImplementedException" errors, empty method stubs, and tightly coupled code that is difficult to maintain and test."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern Example: The Fat Interface"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a multi-function printer system. If we create a single interface for all printer actions, simple machines are forced to implement advanced features."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public interface IMachine {
    void Print(Document d);
    void Fax(Document d);
    void Scan(Document d);
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"If we create a basic printer that can only print, it still has to implement Fax and Scan, violating the ISP."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class SimplePrinter : IMachine {
    public void Print(Document d) {
        // Print logic
    }
    public void Fax(Document d) {
        throw new NotImplementedException();
    }
    public void Scan(Document d) {
        throw new NotImplementedException();
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring with ISP"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fix this, we segregate the fat interface into smaller, client-specific interfaces. Each interface represents a single, cohesive capability."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public interface IPrinter {
    void Print(Document d);
}

public interface IFax {
    void Fax(Document d);
}

public interface IScanner {
    void Scan(Document d);
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Specific Interfaces"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, our simple printer only implements the interface it actually supports, eliminating dead code and empty method implementations."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class SimplePrinter : IPrinter {
    public void Print(Document d) {
        // Print logic only
    }
}

public class MultiFunctionMachine : IPrinter, IFax, IScanner {
    public void Print(Document d) { /* Print */ }
    public void Fax(Document d) { /* Fax */ }
    public void Scan(Document d) { /* Scan */ }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Applying ISP"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose Coupling: Changes to scanning logic do not force recompilation or modification of printing clients."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High Cohesion: Interfaces have a clear, singular purpose focused on specific client needs."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Testability: Mocking smaller interfaces is easier and requires fewer setup steps in unit tests."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Elimination of NotImplementedExceptions: Classes only implement methods they fully support."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interface Segregation Principle guides us to design around the client rather than the implementation. By avoiding fat interfaces and favoring granular, role-specific contracts, software systems become more flexible, resilient to change, and easier to scale over time."}]
